window.PREP_DATA = window.PREP_DATA || {};
window.PREP_DATA.htmlcss = {
  notes: [
    {
      "title": "Semantic HTML",
      "body": "Semantic tags describe meaning, not only look. Use header, nav, main, article, section, aside, footer, button, and label. Screen readers and search engines use that meaning. A page of only divs has no outline. Prefer a real tag when one exists. Default browser behavior, such as submitting a form, also depends on the right tag."
    },
    {
      "title": "Document outline",
      "body": "Headings make the outline of the page. One h1 per page is a good habit. Nest h2 under h1, then h3, in order. Skip levels confuse the outline. Images need alt text that describes the picture. Use alt=\"\" if the image is only decoration so assistive tech can skip it."
    },
    {
      "title": "Forms",
      "body": "Every input needs a label, with for matching id, or by wrapping the input. required, type, autocomplete, and name matter for users and for the data you send. type gives a keyboard and basic checks. name is the key the server receives. autocomplete helps password managers. A button type=\"submit\" sends the form."
    },
    {
      "title": "Box model",
      "body": "A box has content, then padding, then border, then margin. With content-box, width is only the content. With border-box, width includes padding and border. Most sites set box-sizing: border-box on everything. Columns then add up as you expect. Margin is outside the border and can collapse vertically in normal flow."
    },
    {
      "title": "Display & flow",
      "body": "display controls how a box sits in the page. block starts on a new line and takes width. inline sits in text. inline-block sits in a line but accepts width and height. none removes the box. flex and grid are the modern layout tools. position is for exceptions, not for laying out the whole page."
    },
    {
      "title": "Flexbox",
      "body": "Flexbox is one-dimensional layout: a row or a column. The main axis is the direction of flex-direction. justify-content spaces items on the main axis. align-items lines them up on the cross axis. gap is space between items. Prefer gap over margin hacks on flex children."
    },
    {
      "title": "Grid",
      "body": "Grid is two-dimensional: rows and columns together. You define tracks with grid-template-columns and grid-template-rows. minmax, repeat, and auto-fit help galleries. Named areas place pieces of a page. Use grid for page chrome and card galleries. Nest flex inside a grid cell when you need a row of buttons."
    },
    {
      "title": "Responsive",
      "body": "Responsive means the layout works on small and large screens. Write small-screen styles first, then add min-width media queries. Use fluid widths, rem for type, and clamp() for type that grows with the screen. Pick breakpoints from the design, not from a list of phone names. The viewport meta tag makes CSS pixels match the phone width."
    },
    {
      "title": "Specificity",
      "body": "Specificity decides which CSS rule wins when two rules target the same element. Inline style beats id. Id beats class, attribute, and pseudo-class. Those beat element selectors. If scores tie, the later rule wins. Avoid !important except for tiny utilities. Keep selectors short so they are easy to override."
    },
    {
      "title": "Cascade & layers",
      "body": "The cascade is the full order of who wins: origin, importance, layers, specificity, then source order. Origin means browser, user, or author. @layer lets you put resets, components, and utilities in named buckets. A later layer can win over an earlier one even with a weaker selector. That helps when a framework fights your CSS."
    },
    {
      "title": "Accessibility",
      "body": "Accessibility means people can use the page with a keyboard, a screen reader, or low vision. Check color contrast. Keep a visible focus ring. Do not rely on color alone. Respect prefers-reduced-motion. Prefer native controls such as button and input. ARIA adds names and states only when HTML is not enough."
    },
    {
      "title": "Performance",
      "body": "Fewer fonts and compressed images make the first visit faster. Use modern image formats when you can. Load only the CSS you need. Huge unused frameworks cost bytes. Width and height on images reserve space so the page does not jump. Defer scripts so HTML can parse first."
    }
  ],
  questions: [
    { id: 1, level: "beginner", q: "What is semantic HTML?", a: "Semantic HTML means tag names describe meaning, not only how they look. nav, button, article, and table tell screen readers and search engines what a region is for.\n\nA page of only divs has no outline. Default keyboard behavior also follows the real tag.\n\nIn the code: header holds nav with a Home link. main holds an article with an h1 and a paragraph. footer has the year. The structure is the outline of the page.\n\nIf a real tag exists, use it instead of a div with a class that only you understand.", code: "<header> <!-- shows the idea -->\n  <nav><a href=\"/\">Home</a></nav> <!-- the menu -->\n</header>\n<main> <!-- shows the idea -->\n  <article><h1>News</h1><p>Hello</p></article> <!-- shows the idea -->\n</main>\n<footer>© 2026</footer> <!-- shows the idea -->" },
    { id: 2, level: "beginner", q: "div vs span?", a: "div is a block box for grouping. It starts on a new line. span is an inline box for a bit of text inside a sentence.\n\nNeither has meaning. Prefer a real tag when one exists. Do not wrap everything in divs.\n\nIn the code: a div.card wraps a paragraph. span.name wraps Ada inside that sentence. Hello stays inline with the name. The card is the block wrapper.\n\nUse span for a word. Use div only when no semantic tag fits the group.", code: "<!-- Example: div vs span? -->\n<div class=\"card\"> <!-- shows the idea -->\n  <p>Hello <span class=\"name\">Ada</span></p> <!-- shows the idea -->\n</div>" },
    { id: 3, level: "beginner", q: "When do you use a button vs a link?", a: "A link (a href) goes to another place. A button does an action on this page: save, open, delete.\n\nDo not make a div clickable if a button or an a fits. Keyboard and screen readers get the right role for free.\n\nIn the code: About is an a to /about. Save is a button type=\"button\". The comment says compare the two tags. type=\"button\" stops it from submitting a form.\n\nIf it navigates, use a. If it does work here, use button.", code: "<!-- Example: When do you use a button vs a link? -->\n<a href=\"/about\">About</a> <!-- go to another page -->\n<button type=\"button\">Save</button> <!-- do an action -->\n<!-- compare the two tags above -->" },
    { id: 4, level: "beginner", q: "Why do labels matter?", a: "A label names a control. Clicking the label focuses the control. Screen readers announce what the box is for.\n\nUse for=\"id\" or wrap the input in the label. People then know what to type.\n\nIn the code: the first label’s for=\"email\" matches the input id. The second label wraps a checkbox and the text Remember me. Both patterns are valid.\n\nNever leave a placeholder as the only name. Placeholders disappear when the user types.", code: "<label for=\"email\">Email</label> <!-- name the control -->\n<input id=\"email\" type=\"email\" /> <!-- type or pick here -->\n<label> <!-- name the control -->\n  Remember me /* shows the idea */\n  <input type=\"checkbox\" /> <!-- type or pick here -->\n</label>" },
    { id: 5, level: "beginner", q: "What does the alt attribute do?", a: "alt is the text if the picture cannot be seen. Describe the image if it teaches something. Use alt=\"\" if it is only decoration so assistive tech can skip it.\n\nNever omit alt on img. An empty alt is a choice. A missing alt is a mistake.\n\nIn the code: the cat image has a full description. The swirl svg has alt=\"\". The comment says compare the two. Decorative images should not be announced.\n\nIf you delete alt, many readers say the file name. That is worse than alt=\"\".", code: "<!-- Example: What does the alt attribute do? -->\n<img src=\"cat.jpg\" alt=\"An orange cat sleeping on a laptop\" /> <!-- picture plus alt -->\n<img src=\"swirl.svg\" alt=\"\" /> <!-- picture plus alt -->\n<!-- compare the two tags above -->" },
    { id: 6, level: "beginner", q: "What is the difference between id and class?", a: "id should be unique on the page. class can be reused on many elements. Use id for labels and jump links. Prefer class for CSS.\n\nStyles then do not fight over one-off ids.\n\nIn the code: h1 has id=\"title\". Two buttons share class=\"btn\". Save and Cancel look the same. title is unique. btn is shared.\n\nDo not give two elements the same id. CSS and labels will break.", code: "<!-- Example: What is the difference between id and class? -->\n<h1 id=\"title\">Home</h1> <!-- shows the idea -->\n<button class=\"btn\">Save</button> <!-- do an action -->\n<button class=\"btn\">Cancel</button> <!-- do an action -->" },
    { id: 7, level: "beginner", q: "block vs inline vs inline-block?", a: "block takes full width and breaks to a new line. inline sits in text and usually ignores width and height. inline-block sits in a line but accepts width and height.\n\nYou need this before flex and grid, and for small bits of UI.\n\nIn the code: .block, .inline, and .box set those three display values. .box also has width 80px, which inline would ignore. Each class is one rule.\n\nUse inline-block for a chip or icon that needs a size but should sit in a sentence.", code: "/* Example: block vs inline vs inline-block? */\n.block { display: block; } /* one CSS rule */\n.inline { display: inline; } /* one CSS rule */\n.box { display: inline-block; width: 80px; } /* one CSS rule */" },
    { id: 8, level: "beginner", q: "What is the CSS box model?", a: "A box has content, then padding, then border, then margin. With content-box, width is only the content. With border-box, width includes padding and border.\n\nMost sites use border-box so columns add up.\n\nIn the code: * { box-sizing: border-box }. .card is 200px wide including 16px padding and a 2px border. The content area is smaller than 200px.\n\nIf a 200px column overflows, check whether you are still on content-box.", code: "* { box-sizing: border-box; }\n.card { /* shows the idea */\n  width: 200px;      /* includes padding + border */\n  padding: 16px; /* space inside */\n  border: 2px solid #333; /* shows the idea */\n}" },
    { id: 9, level: "beginner", q: "margin vs padding?", a: "Padding is space inside the border. Margin is space outside, pushing neighbors away. Vertical margins can collapse in normal flow.\n\nSpacing that looks right depends on picking the correct one.\n\nIn the code: .card has padding 16px, margin 12px, and a light border. Padding is inside the border. Margin is outside. Both are set on the same class.\n\nUse padding when the background should include the gap. Use margin when the gap should be outside the painted box.", code: ".card { /* shows the idea */\n  padding: 16px;     /* space inside */\n  margin: 12px;      /* space outside */\n  border: 1px solid #ccc; /* shows the idea */\n}" },
    { id: 10, level: "beginner", q: "What is margin collapse?", a: "Adjoining top and bottom margins in normal flow combine to the larger one. That is margin collapse. Padding, border, or flex/grid often stop that.\n\nBeginners are surprised when 20px + 20px is still 20px. Stacked headings and paragraphs show this.\n\nIn the code: .a has margin-bottom 20px. .b has margin-top 20px. The comment says the gap may be 20px, not 40px. .flex uses gap 20px instead.\n\nUse flex or grid gap when you want gaps to add, not collapse.", code: ".a { margin-bottom: 20px; } /* space outside */\n.b { margin-top: 20px; } /* space outside */\n/* in normal flow the gap may be 20px, not 40px */\n.flex { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; } /* one-direction layout */" },
    { id: 11, level: "beginner", q: "relative, absolute, fixed, sticky?", a: "relative nudges from where it already was and stays in flow. absolute jumps out of flow, relative to the nearest positioned ancestor. fixed is usually tied to the viewport. sticky acts relative until it sticks while you scroll.\n\nNav bars and badges use these.\n\nIn the code: .badge is relative. .pin is absolute at top right. .nav is sticky at top 0. The pin is placed inside the relative badge.\n\nIf absolute looks wrong, the ancestor may still be position static.", code: "/* Example: relative, absolute, fixed, sticky? */\n.badge { position: relative; } /* one CSS rule */\n.pin { position: absolute; top: 0; right: 0; } /* lift out of flow */\n.nav { position: sticky; top: 0; } /* stick while scrolling */" },
    { id: 12, level: "beginner", q: "What does position: static mean?", a: "static is the default: sit in normal flow. top and left do nothing. A positioned ancestor is one whose position is not static.\n\nAbsolute children look for that ancestor.\n\nIn the code: .plain is static. .wrap is relative, so it becomes a containing block. .child is absolute at top 8px, placed against .wrap if it lives inside.\n\nDo not set top on a static box. It will not move.", code: "/* Example: What does position: static mean? */\n.plain { position: static; } /* default */\n.wrap { position: relative; } /* now a containing block */\n.child { position: absolute; top: 8px; } /* lift out of flow */" },
    { id: 13, level: "beginner", q: "What is z-index?", a: "z-index is who sits on top, like layers. It only compares inside the same stacking context. A parent with transform can trap a huge z-index.\n\nDropdowns and modals hit this.\n\nIn the code: .back is z-index 1. .front is z-index 2. Both are position relative. Front paints above back if they overlap.\n\nIf z-index 999 does nothing, look for a parent that created a new stacking context.", code: "/* Example: What is z-index? */\n.back { position: relative; z-index: 1; } /* who sits on top */\n.front { position: relative; z-index: 2; } /* who sits on top */\n/* compare the two rules above */" },
    { id: 14, level: "beginner", q: "How do you center a div?", a: "Flex or grid with centering is the usual answer. place-items: center on grid is short. A block with a width can use margin-left/right auto.\n\nSkip old negative-margin hacks.\n\nIn the code: .stage is display grid, place-items center, min-height 100vh. The child sits in the middle of the viewport. One rule set does both axes.\n\nUse grid place-items when you want horizontal and vertical center together.", code: ".stage { /* shows the idea */\n  display: grid; /* rows and columns */\n  place-items: center; /* horizontal + vertical */\n  min-height: 100vh; /* shows the idea */\n}" },
    { id: 15, level: "beginner", q: "px vs em vs rem vs %?", a: "px is a fixed CSS pixel. rem is relative to the root font size, good for type. em is relative to the parent font. % is relative to the containing block, or the font for font-size.\n\nZoom and responsive type depend on this.\n\nIn the code: html font-size is 16px. h1 is 2rem, so 32px if root is 16. .pad padding is 1em. .col is width 50%. Each unit is shown once.\n\nUse rem for font-size so user zoom still scales the page.", code: "html { font-size: 16px; } /* text size */\nh1 { font-size: 2rem; }     /* 32px if root is 16 */\n.pad { padding: 1em; }      /* based on this element's font */\n.col { width: 50%; } /* one CSS rule */" },
    { id: 16, level: "intermediate", q: "What is specificity?", a: "Inline style beats id. Id beats class. Class beats element. If scores tie, the later rule wins. !important beats normal rules but is hard to override.\n\nKeep selectors short.\n\nIn the code: p is gray (element). .text is blue (class wins). #hero is navy (id wins). The comments label each level.\n\nIf a class “does nothing,” an id or inline style is probably winning.", code: "/* Example: What is specificity? */\np { color: gray; }           /* element */\n.text { color: blue; }       /* class wins over element */\n#hero { color: navy; }       /* id wins over class */" },
    { id: 17, level: "intermediate", q: "How does the cascade work?", a: "The cascade order is origin (browser, user, author), then importance, then layers, then specificity, then source order. Understanding this beats writing longer selectors.\n\nA framework fighting your CSS is a cascade problem.\n\nIn the code: @layer lists reset, components, utilities. components sets .btn padding 8px. utilities sets .p-0 padding 0. The later layer can win. The utilities file is the last layer.\n\nPut overrides in a later layer instead of raising specificity.", code: "@layer reset, components, utilities; /* shows the idea */\n@layer components { /* shows the idea */\n  .btn { padding: 8px; } /* space inside */\n}\n@layer utilities { /* shows the idea */\n  .p-0 { padding: 0; } /* later layer can win */" },
    { id: 18, level: "intermediate", q: "What are stacking contexts?", a: "Some properties create a new stacking world: opacity less than 1, transform, filter, position plus z-index, isolation. Children cannot climb out of that world to beat a sibling of the parent.\n\nModals stuck under a header often live in the wrong context.\n\nIn the code: .header has transform and z-index 1, which makes a context. .modal is position fixed z-index 999. If modal is inside header, 999 is trapped.\n\nPortal the modal to body, or remove the transform on the parent.", code: ".header { /* shows the idea */\n  transform: translateZ(0); /* new stacking context */\n  z-index: 1; /* who sits on top */\n}\n.modal { position: fixed; z-index: 999; } /* who sits on top */" },
    { id: 19, level: "intermediate", q: "flex vs grid?", a: "Flex is one direction: a row of buttons or a column of items. Grid is rows and columns together: page chrome, galleries. You can nest them.\n\nDo not force a table with flex hacks.\n\nIn the code: .row is flex with gap 8px. .gallery is grid with three 1fr columns and gap 16px. Flex for a line. Grid for a table of cards.\n\nPick flex for a toolbar. Pick grid for a two-dimensional page.", code: ".row { display: flex; gap: 8px; } /* one-direction layout */\n.gallery { /* shows the idea */\n  display: grid; /* rows and columns */\n  grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr 1fr; /* column sizes */\n  gap: 16px; /* shows the idea */\n}" },
    { id: 20, level: "intermediate", q: "justify-content vs align-items vs align-content?", a: "justify-content spreads items on the main axis. align-items lines them up on the cross axis. align-content is extra space between wrapped lines.\n\nA nav that is not centered the way you meant usually mixed these up.\n\nIn the code: .bar is flex. space-between is on the main axis. center is on the cross axis. The bar is a row by default.\n\nIf items sit on the wrong axis, check flex-direction first.", code: ".bar { /* shows the idea */\n  display: flex; /* one-direction layout */\n  justify-content: space-between; /* main axis */\n  align-items: center;            /* cross axis */\n}" },
    { id: 21, level: "intermediate", q: "flex-grow, shrink, basis?", a: "basis is the starting size. grow shares leftover space. shrink gives space back when tight. 0 0 auto means size to content, do not flex.\n\nSidebars that should not squash use a fixed basis and grow 0.\n\nIn the code: .main is flex 1 1 auto, so it grows and shrinks. .side is 0 0 240px, a fixed starting width. Compare the two rules.\n\nIf a sidebar shrinks, set shrink to 0 and a basis in px.", code: "/* Example: flex-grow, shrink, basis? */\n.main { flex: 1 1 auto; }   /* grow and shrink */\n.side { flex: 0 0 240px; }  /* fixed starting width */\n/* compare the two rules above */" },
    { id: 22, level: "intermediate", q: "minmax and auto-fit?", a: "repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr)) makes as many columns as fit. Each column is at least 240px and can grow.\n\nCard galleries use this without many breakpoints.\n\nIn the code: .cards is grid with that repeat minmax pattern and gap 16px. Columns wrap as the screen shrinks. 1fr shares leftover space.\n\nIf a column goes under 240px, the browser wraps to fewer columns.", code: ".cards { /* shows the idea */\n  display: grid; /* rows and columns */\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr)); /* column sizes */\n  gap: 16px; /* shows the idea */\n}" },
    { id: 23, level: "intermediate", q: "fr unit?", a: "fr is a share of leftover space in grid. 1fr 2fr splits remaining space 1:2 after fixed tracks and gaps.\n\nSidebar plus fluid main uses this.\n\nIn the code: .page columns are 200px, then 1fr, then 2fr. The first track is fixed. The other two split leftover 1:2. display is grid.\n\nPut fixed tracks first, then fr tracks for what should grow.", code: ".page { /* shows the idea */\n  display: grid; /* rows and columns */\n  grid-template-columns: 200px 1fr 2fr; /* fixed, then 1:2 leftover */\n}" },
    { id: 24, level: "intermediate", q: "What is a media query?", a: "A media query applies rules when a condition is true, such as @media (min-width: 768px). Prefer min-width (mobile first). Also consider prefers-reduced-motion and prefers-color-scheme.\n\nPhones versus desktops is the usual case.\n\nIn the code: .nav is a column by default. At min-width 768px it becomes a row. The query wraps the row rule. Small screens keep the column.\n\nWrite the small layout first, then add min-width overrides.", code: ".nav { flex-direction: column; } /* one CSS rule */\n@media (min-width: 768px) { /* shows the idea */\n  .nav { flex-direction: row; } /* one CSS rule */\n}" },
    { id: 25, level: "intermediate", q: "mobile-first CSS?", a: "Mobile-first means write the small-screen styles as the default. Then add extras at larger min-width breakpoints. Do not ship a huge desktop DOM and only hide it with display:none if you can avoid that.\n\nPhones on slow networks feel this.\n\nIn the code: .hero is 1.25rem by default. At 900px it becomes 2rem. The extra is only for large screens. The small size is the base.\n\nAdd properties at larger breakpoints. Do not start with desktop and undo it.", code: ".hero { font-size: 1.25rem; } /* text size */\n@media (min-width: 900px) { /* shows the idea */\n  .hero { font-size: 2rem; } /* extra for large screens */\n}" },
    { id: 26, level: "intermediate", q: "What is the viewport meta tag?", a: "width=device-width makes CSS pixels match the phone width. Without it, phones pretend to be about 980px and shrink the page.\n\nMedia queries then actually match the phone.\n\nIn the code: the viewport meta is in head. content lists width=device-width and initial-scale=1. That is the usual mobile tag.\n\nIf your breakpoints never fire on a phone, this tag is probably missing.", code: "<!-- Example: What is the viewport meta tag? -->\n<head> <!-- shows the idea -->\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\" /> <!-- info for the browser -->\n</head>" },
    { id: 27, level: "intermediate", q: "relative vs absolute units in responsive design?", a: "rem, %, vw, and fr help layouts grow. px is fine for hairline borders. clamp can fluidly size type between min and max.\n\nZoom and small screens still work.\n\nIn the code: .title uses clamp for font-size. .wrap is min(100%, 70rem). .line is a 1px border. Fluid type, capped width, thin line.\n\nUse px for 1px borders. Use rem and clamp for type.", code: "/* Example: relative vs absolute units in responsive design? */\n.title { font-size: clamp(1rem, 2vw + 0.8rem, 1.5rem); } /* text size */\n.wrap { width: min(100%, 70rem); } /* one CSS rule */\n.line { border-bottom: 1px solid #ccc; } /* one CSS rule */" },
    { id: 28, level: "intermediate", q: "What is clamp()?", a: "clamp(min, preferred, max) picks the middle when it fits, else the edges. Popular for fluid type.\n\nText is then neither tiny nor huge.\n\nIn the code: h1 font-size is clamp(1rem, 2vw + 0.8rem, 1.4rem). Preferred grows with the viewport. Min and max cap it. One property does the range.\n\nIf type explodes on a wide monitor, lower the max in clamp.", code: "/* Example: What is clamp()? */\nh1 { /* shows the idea */\n  font-size: clamp(1rem, 2vw + 0.8rem, 1.4rem); /* text size */\n}" },
    { id: 29, level: "intermediate", q: "pseudo-class vs pseudo-element?", a: "A pseudo-class is a state: hover, focus, nth-child. A pseudo-element styles a part: ::before, ::after, ::placeholder. Old single-colon syntax still works for some.\n\nTarget the right thing.\n\nIn the code: .btn:hover sets background. .btn::before sets content to an arrow. Hover is a state. ::before is a generated bit of content.\n\nUse : for states. Use :: for extra pieces of the box.", code: "/* Example: pseudo-class vs pseudo-element? */\n.btn:hover { background: #111; } /* one CSS rule */\n.btn::before { content: \"→ \"; } /* one CSS rule */\n/* compare the two rules above */" },
    { id: 30, level: "beginner", q: "What is ::before used for?", a: "::before inserts extra content with the content property. Good for decorative icons. Not a replacement for real text users must read or copy.\n\nAccessibility depends on real text in the HTML.\n\nIn the code: .note::before has content \"Note: \" and font-weight 700. The word Note is generated. It is not in the HTML.\n\nIf the word must be read and copied, put it in the HTML, not in ::before.", code: ".note::before { /* shows the idea */\n  content: \"Note: \"; /* shows the idea */\n  font-weight: 700; /* shows the idea */\n}" },
    { id: 31, level: "intermediate", q: ":nth-child vs :nth-of-type?", a: ":nth-child counts all children. :nth-of-type counts among siblings of that tag only. An extra span can throw off nth-child.\n\nStriped tables and lists use these.\n\nIn the code: li:nth-child(odd) stripes list items. p:nth-of-type(2) bolds the second p among p siblings. Compare the two rules.\n\nIf stripes skip a row, you probably counted a non-li child. Use nth-of-type or a class.", code: "/* Example: :nth-child vs :nth-of-type? */\nli:nth-child(odd) { background: #f4f4f4; } /* one CSS rule */\np:nth-of-type(2) { font-weight: 700; } /* one CSS rule */\n/* compare the two rules above */" },
    { id: 32, level: "intermediate", q: "What is the difference between :focus and :focus-visible?", a: ":focus matches any focus, including mouse clicks. :focus-visible is mainly for keyboard (and some browser guesses). Use :focus-visible for outlines so mouse users are not annoyed, keyboard users still see a ring.\n\nNever remove outlines with no replacement.\n\nIn the code: .btn:focus-visible has a 3px outline and 2px offset. Keyboard tabbers see the ring. The outline is the visible focus.\n\noutline: none with no other ring hides the keyboard path.", code: ".btn:focus-visible { /* shows the idea */\n  outline: 3px solid #06c; /* shows the idea */\n  outline-offset: 2px; /* shows the idea */\n}" },
    { id: 33, level: "intermediate", q: "outline vs border?", a: "Outline does not take space in the box model. It is the usual focus ring. Border takes space and changes layout.\n\nDo not remove outlines without a replacement. Keyboard users need a ring.\n\nIn the code: .card has a 1px border. :focus-visible adds a 2px outline. Border is always there. Outline appears on keyboard focus.\n\nIf a focus ring shifts layout, you used border. Use outline.", code: ".card { border: 1px solid #ddd; } /* one CSS rule */\n.card:focus-visible { /* shows the idea */\n  outline: 2px solid #06c; /* shows the idea */\n}" },
    { id: 34, level: "beginner", q: "How do you hide content accessibly?", a: "A visually-hidden class keeps text for screen readers. display:none and visibility:hidden remove it from assistive tech too. aria-hidden=\"true\" hides from AT but not from sight.\n\nSkip links and icon-only buttons use the visually-hidden pattern.\n\nIn the code: .sr-only is position absolute, 1px size, overflow hidden, clip rect. Sighted users do not see it. Screen readers still read it.\n\nDo not use display:none for “hidden but announced” text.", code: ".sr-only { /* shows the idea */\n  position: absolute; /* lift out of flow */\n  width: 1px; /* shows the idea */\n  height: 1px; /* shows the idea */\n  overflow: hidden; /* shows the idea */\n  clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0); /* shows the idea */\n}" },
    { id: 35, level: "intermediate", q: "What is aria and when do you need it?", a: "ARIA adds names and states when HTML is not enough. First rule: do not use ARIA if a native element works. Wrong ARIA is worse than none.\n\nCustom widgets need it. A real button does not.\n\nIn the code: a button has aria-expanded=\"false\" and aria-controls=\"menu\". The ul#menu is hidden. Expanded would flip to true when open. Native button plus ARIA state.\n\nIf you can use details/summary or a real button, do that first.", code: "<button type=\"button\" aria-expanded=\"false\" aria-controls=\"menu\"> <!-- do an action -->\n  Menu /* shows the idea */\n</button>\n<ul id=\"menu\" hidden><li><a href=\"/\">Home</a></li></ul> <!-- shows the idea -->" },
    { id: 36, level: "intermediate", q: "role, aria-label, aria-labelledby?", a: "role changes how assistive tech maps the control. aria-label is a string name. aria-labelledby points at visible text ids. Prefer labelledby when the text is on screen.\n\nNames then stay in sync with what people see.\n\nIn the code: a dialog has aria-labelledby=\"t\" and aria-modal=\"true\". h2#t is Delete file?. Cancel is a button. The heading is the accessible name.\n\nIf you copy a new title into the h2, labelledby still works. A duplicate aria-label can drift.", code: "<div role=\"dialog\" aria-labelledby=\"t\" aria-modal=\"true\"> <!-- shows the idea -->\n  <h2 id=\"t\">Delete file?</h2> <!-- shows the idea -->\n  <button type=\"button\">Cancel</button> <!-- do an action -->\n</div>" },
    { id: 37, level: "beginner", q: "Why use a table for tabular data?", a: "Tables have headers, rows, and a keyboard model. Do not use tables for page layout. Use th and scope for simple tables.\n\nSpreadsheets of data belong in a table. Posters do not.\n\nIn the code: the first row has th scope=\"col\" for Name and Score. Ada’s row has th scope=\"row\" and td 98. Headers are marked. Data is in td.\n\nIf you only need columns of cards, use grid, not table.", code: "<table> <!-- shows the idea -->\n  <tr><th scope=\"col\">Name</th><th scope=\"col\">Score</th></tr> <!-- shows the idea -->\n  <tr><th scope=\"row\">Ada</th><td>98</td></tr> <!-- shows the idea -->\n</table>" },
    { id: 38, level: "beginner", q: "What is srcset used for?", a: "srcset offers different files for density or width. sizes tells the browser how wide the image will be. The browser picks a file so you save bandwidth.\n\nPhones then do not download a huge desktop photo.\n\nIn the code: src is cat-400.jpg. srcset lists 400w and 800w. sizes is 400px above 600px, else 100vw. alt is A cat.\n\nIf sizes is wrong, the browser may pick a file that is too large.", code: "<img <!-- picture plus alt -->\n  src=\"cat-400.jpg\" /* shows the idea */\n  srcset=\"cat-400.jpg 400w, cat-800.jpg 800w\" /* shows the idea */\n  sizes=\"(min-width: 600px) 400px, 100vw\" /* shows the idea */\n  alt=\"A cat\" /* shows the idea */\n/>" },
    { id: 39, level: "intermediate", q: "img vs background-image?", a: "img is content: it has alt and loads as a replaced element. CSS backgrounds are decoration. If the picture matters, use img.\n\nSEO and accessibility follow the img.\n\nIn the code: hero.jpg is an img with alt Team photo. .hero uses background-image dots.svg as decoration. The photo is content. The dots are wallpaper.\n\nDo not put a meaningful photo only in background-image. It will have no alt.", code: "<img src=\"hero.jpg\" alt=\"Team photo\" /> <!-- picture plus alt -->\n.hero { /* shows the idea */\n  background-image: url(\"dots.svg\"); /* decoration */\n}" },
    { id: 40, level: "intermediate", q: "object-fit?", a: "object-fit: cover crops to fill. contain letterboxes. fill stretches. Used on img and video in a fixed box.\n\nAvatars and thumbnails use cover.\n\nIn the code: .thumb is 120×120 with object-fit cover. The image fills the square and extra is cropped. Width and height lock the box.\n\nIf faces get cropped, switch to contain or change the image.", code: ".thumb { /* shows the idea */\n  width: 120px; /* shows the idea */\n  height: 120px; /* shows the idea */\n  object-fit: cover; /* fill the square, crop extra */\n}" },
    { id: 41, level: "beginner", q: "absolute vs relative URL?", a: "An absolute URL can include the site and path, or at least a path from the site root. A relative URL is based on the current page path. Site-root paths start with /.\n\nImages 404 after you move a page if the path was relative to the old folder.\n\nIn the code: the first a is a full https URL. the second is /about from the site root. the img src is cat.jpg, relative to the current page. Three styles.\n\nUse /images/cat.jpg if the file always lives at the site root.", code: "<!-- Example: absolute vs relative URL? -->\n<a href=\"https://example.com/a\">Absolute</a>\n<a href=\"/about\">From site root</a> <!-- go to another page -->\n<img src=\"cat.jpg\" alt=\"Cat\" /> <!-- picture plus alt -->" },
    { id: 42, level: "intermediate", q: "What is the difference between <script> defer and async?", a: "defer and async both download while HTML parses. defer runs in order after the document is parsed. async runs whenever it is ready, order not guaranteed.\n\nES modules defer by default. The page is not blank waiting for JS.\n\nIn the code: a.js has defer. b.js has async. Compare the two tags. defer keeps order. async does not.\n\nUse defer for scripts that need the DOM and a known order. Use async for independent analytics.", code: "<!-- Example: What is the difference between <script> defer and async? -->\n<script src=\"a.js\" defer></script> <!-- load JavaScript -->\n<script src=\"b.js\" async></script> <!-- runs when download finishes -->\n<!-- compare the two tags above -->" },
    { id: 43, level: "beginner", q: "What does <meta charset='UTF-8'> do?", a: "charset UTF-8 says these bytes are Unicode text. Put it early in head so characters do not look like garbage.\n\nNames, emoji, and other languages need this.\n\nIn the code: meta charset is the first tag inside head. title is Preplace. UTF-8 is declared before the title text.\n\nIf you put charset late, the title can already be decoded wrong.", code: "<head> <!-- shows the idea -->\n  <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" /> <!-- info for the browser -->\n  <title>Preplace</title> <!-- shows the idea -->\n</head>" },
    { id: 44, level: "beginner", q: "block, inline, and replaced elements?", a: "Replaced elements like img, video, and iframe get their size from an outside resource. width and height attributes reserve space so the page does not jump.\n\nThat is layout shift.\n\nIn the code: img has width 800 height 400. video has 640×360 and controls. Compare the two tags. The box is reserved before the file arrives.\n\nAlways set width and height on images, even if CSS later sets width 100%.", code: "<!-- Example: block, inline, and replaced elements? -->\n<img src=\"hero.jpg\" width=\"800\" height=\"400\" alt=\"Hero\" /> <!-- picture plus alt -->\n<video width=\"640\" height=\"360\" controls></video> <!-- shows the idea -->\n<!-- compare the two tags above -->" },
    { id: 45, level: "intermediate", q: "What is CLS and how does HTML help?", a: "Cumulative Layout Shift is how much the page jumps while loading. Reserve space with width/height or aspect-ratio on images and embeds.\n\nUsers and Core Web Vitals both care.\n\nIn the code: img is width 100%, height auto, aspect-ratio 16/9. The box is reserved. The image can still scale. The ratio holds the height.\n\nIf a banner pops in and shoves the article down, you forgot to reserve space.", code: "img { /* shows the idea */\n  width: 100%; /* shows the idea */\n  height: auto; /* shows the idea */\n  aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; /* reserve the box */\n}" },
    { id: 46, level: "intermediate", q: "aspect-ratio property?", a: "aspect-ratio keeps width and height in proportion as the box resizes. Great for videos and cards. Combine with object-fit for media.\n\nThumbnails stay 16:9.\n\nIn the code: .video is aspect-ratio 16/9 and width 100%. Height follows the ratio. The shape stays movie-like.\n\nIf a card squishes, set aspect-ratio instead of a fixed height only.", code: ".video { /* shows the idea */\n  aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; /* keep the shape */\n  width: 100%; /* shows the idea */\n}" },
    { id: 47, level: "intermediate", q: "What is BEM?", a: "BEM is Block__Element--Modifier. Class names stay clear and low-specificity. Example: card__title--large. Utility-first (Tailwind) is another convention.\n\nTeams need one naming plan.\n\nIn the code: article is card card--featured. h2 is card__title. Hello is the title. The block is card. The element is title. featured is a modifier.\n\nDo not nest .card .title in CSS if the class is already card__title.", code: "<!-- Example: What is BEM? -->\n<article class=\"card card--featured\"> <!-- shows the idea -->\n  <h2 class=\"card__title\">Hello</h2> <!-- shows the idea -->\n</article>" },
    { id: 48, level: "intermediate", q: "CSS modules vs global CSS?", a: "CSS modules hash class names per file so they do not clash. Global CSS is simpler for tiny pages and resets. Many apps use both.\n\nBig React apps need unique names.\n\nIn the code: Button.module.css has .btn { padding: 8px }. In JSX, styles.btn becomes something like btn_x7k2. The hash is unique.\n\nIf two global .btn rules fight, move them into modules or rename.", code: "/* Example: CSS modules vs global CSS? */\n/* Button.module.css */\n.btn { padding: 8px; } /* space inside */\n/* in JSX: className={styles.btn} becomes btn_x7k2 */" },
    { id: 49, level: "intermediate", q: "What is the difference between visibility, opacity, and display?", a: "display:none removes from layout and the accessibility tree. visibility:hidden hides but keeps the space. opacity:0 hides visually but can still receive clicks unless pointer-events:none.\n\nMenus and loaders need the right one.\n\nIn the code: .gone is display none. .invisible is visibility hidden. .ghost is opacity 0 and pointer-events none. Three kinds of hide.\n\nIf a hidden overlay still steals clicks, you used opacity without pointer-events none.", code: "/* Example: What is the difference between visibility, opacity, and display? */\n.gone { display: none; } /* one CSS rule */\n.invisible { visibility: hidden; } /* one CSS rule */\n.ghost { opacity: 0; pointer-events: none; } /* one CSS rule */" },
    { id: 50, level: "intermediate", q: "pointer-events?", a: "pointer-events: none lets clicks pass through. Useful on decorative overlay parts. Do not disable events on real controls.\n\nStacked UI uses this.\n\nIn the code: .overlay-art is pointer-events none. .overlay-art button is pointer-events auto. Clicks go through the art. The real button still works.\n\nIf a decorative layer blocks a button, set none on the layer and auto on the control.", code: ".overlay-art { /* shows the idea */\n  pointer-events: none; /* clicks go through */\n}\n.overlay-art button { /* shows the idea */\n  pointer-events: auto; /* the real button still works */\n}" },
    { id: 51, level: "advanced", q: "What is contain and content-visibility?", a: "contain limits layout and paint work to a subtree. content-visibility: auto can skip rendering off-screen stuff for a faster first paint. Use contain-intrinsic-size so scroll does not jump.\n\nLong pages benefit.\n\nIn the code: .card has content-visibility auto and contain-intrinsic-size 0 240px. Off-screen cards can skip work. 240px is the guessed height.\n\nIf the scrollbar jumps, raise contain-intrinsic-size to match real card height.", code: ".card { /* shows the idea */\n  content-visibility: auto; /* shows the idea */\n  contain-intrinsic-size: 0 240px; /* shows the idea */\n}" },
    { id: 52, level: "advanced", q: "What is a stacking context interview example?", a: "A modal with z-index 999 inside a header with transform stays trapped. A sibling dropdown from another context can cover it. Fix by rendering the modal at the root (portal) or restructuring.\n\nThis is a real stacking bug, not a bigger number problem.\n\nIn the code: .header has transform translateZ(0). .modal is fixed z-index 999. The comment says move .modal to body. Transform on header is the trap.\n\nIf raising z-index fails, check ancestors for transform, opacity, or filter.", code: "/* Example: What is a stacking context interview example? */\n.header { transform: translateZ(0); } /* one CSS rule */\n.modal { position: fixed; z-index: 999; } /* who sits on top */\n/* move .modal to body so it is not trapped */" },
    { id: 53, level: "intermediate", q: "how to make a sticky header?", a: "position: sticky; top: 0 makes a sticky header. No ancestor should have overflow hidden that kills the sticky box. Give it a z-index so content slides underneath.\n\nLong articles use this.\n\nIn the code: header is sticky, top 0, z-index 10, background white. It sticks to the top as you scroll. White background covers text sliding under.\n\nIf sticky does nothing, search ancestors for overflow hidden.", code: "header { /* shows the idea */\n  position: sticky; /* stick while scrolling */\n  top: 0; /* shows the idea */\n  z-index: 10; /* who sits on top */\n  background: white; /* shows the idea */\n}" },
    { id: 54, level: "intermediate", q: "overflow hidden and sticky/fixed bugs?", a: "Overflow other than visible on an ancestor can make sticky fail. It can also create a new scroll container. Check the whole ancestor chain.\n\nA pretty card that clips a dropdown is the same family of bug.\n\nIn the code: .clip is overflow hidden and can break sticky inside. .safe is overflow visible. .stick is sticky top 0. Compare clip vs safe.\n\nWhen sticky fails, log computed overflow on each parent until you find hidden or auto.", code: "/* Example: overflow hidden and sticky/fixed bugs? */\n.clip { overflow: hidden; } /* can break sticky inside */\n.safe { overflow: visible; } /* one CSS rule */\n.stick { position: sticky; top: 0; } /* stick while scrolling */" },
    { id: 55, level: "beginner", q: "How do you create a CSS triangle?", a: "A zero-size box with transparent borders and one colored border makes a triangle. It is a trick. SVG is often clearer in production.\n\nYou may still be asked to draw one in CSS.\n\nIn the code: .tri has width 0 height 0. left and right borders are transparent 8px. bottom is 12px solid #333. The visible point is the colored border.\n\nUse SVG for real icons. Use this trick only when you must.", code: ".tri { /* shows the idea */\n  width: 0; /* shows the idea */\n  height: 0; /* shows the idea */\n  border-left: 8px solid transparent; /* shows the idea */\n  border-right: 8px solid transparent; /* shows the idea */\n  border-bottom: 12px solid #333; /* shows the idea */\n}" },
    { id: 56, level: "intermediate", q: "custom properties?", a: "Custom properties are CSS variables. --name stores a value. var(--name, fallback) reads it. They cascade and can change per component or theme.\n\nThey are not Sass variables, which exist only at compile time. Dark mode uses them.\n\nIn the code: :root sets --bg and --fg. .card uses var(--bg) and var(--fg, #111). The fallback is #111 if --fg is missing.\n\nChange --bg on a parent, and children update without rewriting each rule.", code: ":root { --bg: white; --fg: #111; } /* one CSS rule */\n.card { /* shows the idea */\n  background: var(--bg); /* shows the idea */\n  color: var(--fg, #111); /* shows the idea */\n}" },
    { id: 57, level: "intermediate", q: "Sass vs CSS variables?", a: "Sass gives mixins and loops before the CSS exists. CSS variables can change in the browser, even in media queries. Many teams use both.\n\nTheming at runtime needs CSS variables.\n\nIn the code: :root has --accent. In prefers-color-scheme dark, --accent becomes #8cf. That is a runtime swap. No Sass compile step in this snippet.\n\nUse CSS variables for values that change with theme or container.", code: ":root { --accent: #06c; } /* one CSS rule */\n@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { /* shows the idea */\n  :root { --accent: #8cf; } /* runtime swap */\n}" },
    { id: 58, level: "intermediate", q: "What is @supports?", a: "@supports (display: grid) wraps CSS that only runs if the browser understands it. That is progressive enhancement.\n\nOld browsers keep the simpler layout.\n\nIn the code: .layout is display block first. Inside @supports (display: grid) it becomes grid with gap. Browsers without grid ignore the inner block.\n\nAlways provide the simple rule first, then enhance inside @supports.", code: ".layout { display: block; } /* one CSS rule */\n@supports (display: grid) { /* rows and columns */\n  .layout { display: grid; gap: 16px; } /* rows and columns */\n}" },
    { id: 59, level: "beginner", q: "What is progressive enhancement?", a: "Progressive enhancement means core content works with basic HTML first. Then CSS and JS add layout and behavior. The opposite is a JS-only blank page.\n\nWhen scripts fail, the story is still there.\n\nIn the code: h1 News, a paragraph of story, and a Like button. The story is in HTML. Like can gain JS later. No CSS is required to read the news.\n\nPut the text in HTML. Do not wait for JavaScript to insert the article.", code: "<!-- Example: What is progressive enhancement? -->\n<h1>News</h1> <!-- shows the idea -->\n<p>The story is here even without CSS.</p> <!-- shows the idea -->\n<button type=\"button\">Like</button> <!-- do an action -->" },
    { id: 60, level: "intermediate", q: "critical CSS?", a: "Critical CSS is the CSS needed for the first screen, often inlined to avoid an extra wait. The rest loads later. Easy to over-engineer; measure.\n\nFirst paint can get faster.\n\nIn the code: a style tag sets header background and color. Then a link loads full.css. The header paints from the inline block. The rest arrives after.\n\nInline only a small first-screen set. Huge inline CSS hurts caching.", code: "<style> <!-- shows the idea -->\n  header { background: #111; color: #fff; } /* one CSS rule */\n</style>\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"full.css\" /> <!-- load a stylesheet -->" },
    { id: 61, level: "intermediate", q: "render-blocking resources?", a: "Render-blocking resources delay first paint. CSS in the head waits so you do not see unstyled HTML. Heavy fonts and sync scripts delay rendering. defer/async and font-display help.\n\nSpeed depends on what you put in head.\n\nIn the code: app.css is a stylesheet link. app.js has defer. Compare the two tags. CSS blocks. The script waits until parse is done.\n\nDo not put a large sync script in head without defer or async.", code: "<!-- Example: render-blocking resources? -->\n<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"app.css\" /> <!-- load a stylesheet -->\n<script src=\"app.js\" defer></script> <!-- load JavaScript -->\n<!-- compare the two tags above -->" },
    { id: 62, level: "beginner", q: "What is FOUC?", a: "FOUC is Flash of Unstyled Content: HTML shows before CSS applies. Fonts have related flashes. font-display: swap shows fallback text faster.\n\nPerceived quality drops when the page looks naked for a moment.\n\nIn the code: @font-face names Brand, loads woff2, and sets font-display swap. Fallback text appears, then Brand swaps in. The face is defined, not applied to body here.\n\nUse swap so text is readable before the font file arrives.", code: "@font-face { /* shows the idea */\n  font-family: Brand; /* shows the idea */\n  src: url(\"brand.woff2\") format(\"woff2\"); /* shows the idea */\n  font-display: swap; /* shows the idea */\n}" },
    { id: 63, level: "intermediate", q: "system fonts vs web fonts?", a: "System fonts are instant and free. Web fonts brand the site but cost bytes and can shift layout. Subset and preload the most important face.\n\nPerformance vs brand is the trade.\n\nIn the code: body font-family is system-ui, Segoe UI, sans-serif. No extra file download. The stack is system first.\n\nIf branding needs a custom face, load one weight, not the whole family.", code: "/* Example: system fonts vs web fonts? */\nbody { /* shows the idea */\n  font-family: system-ui, \"Segoe UI\", sans-serif; /* shows the idea */\n}" },
    { id: 64, level: "beginner", q: "How do you include a stylesheet?", a: "link rel=\"stylesheet\" in head is how CSS gets on the page. @import inside CSS adds extra delay. Inline style attributes are for one-offs.\n\nStyles then load early.\n\nIn the code: head contains link rel stylesheet href styles.css. That is the usual include. No @import here.\n\nPut the link in head. Do not @import the main file from another CSS file.", code: "<!-- Example: How do you include a stylesheet? -->\n<head> <!-- shows the idea -->\n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\" /> <!-- load a stylesheet -->\n</head>" },
    { id: 65, level: "intermediate", q: "specificity of inline styles vs !important?", a: "Inline styles beat classes. !important in a stylesheet can beat a normal inline style. An inline style with !important is very hard to override. Avoid it.\n\nDebugging “why will this not change” often lands here.\n\nIn the code: the p has style color red. .note is color blue !important. The comment says compare. !important can win over the inline color.\n\nRemove !important instead of adding a second one.", code: "<!-- Example: specificity of inline styles vs !important? -->\n<p style=\"color: red\">Hi</p> <!-- shows the idea -->\n.note { color: blue !important; } /* one CSS rule */\n<!-- compare the two tags above -->" },
    { id: 66, level: "intermediate", q: "attribute selectors?", a: "Attribute selectors match HTML attributes, such as [type=\"email\"]. They have class-level specificity. Useful for data-state. Can be slower if overused on huge DOMs.\n\nForms and components use them.\n\nIn the code: input[type=\"email\"] sets border color. [data-state=\"open\"] is display block. Compare the two rules. One uses type. One uses data-state.\n\nPrefer a class for hot paths. Use attributes for state that already lives in HTML.", code: "/* Example: attribute selectors? */\ninput[type=\"email\"] { border-color: #06c; } /* one CSS rule */\n[data-state=\"open\"] { display: block; } /* one CSS rule */\n/* compare the two rules above */" },
    { id: 67, level: "beginner", q: "descendant vs child vs sibling selectors?", a: "A space means any descendant. > means a direct child. + is the next sibling. ~ is later siblings. Prefer shallow selectors.\n\nStyles then do not leak too far.\n\nIn the code: .card p is any p inside. .card > p is only a direct child. h2 + p is the p right after h2. h2 ~ p is p siblings after h2.\n\nIf a nested p gets styled by accident, you used a descendant space instead of >.", code: ".card p { }      /* any p inside */\n.card > p { }    /* only direct child */\nh2 + p { }       /* p right after h2 */\nh2 ~ p { }       /* p siblings after h2 */" },
    { id: 68, level: "intermediate", q: "Why is * { margin: 0 } a reset debate?", a: "* { margin: 0 } is easy and a bit costly. Modern resets are more targeted. A common pattern: html { box-sizing: border-box } and * { box-sizing: inherit }.\n\nConsistency without styling every element to zero.\n\nIn the code: html sets border-box. *, *::before, *::after inherit box-sizing. The universal selector is used only for box-sizing. Margins are not zeroed here.\n\nInherit box-sizing so a widget can opt out on one subtree.", code: "html { box-sizing: border-box; } /* one CSS rule */\n*, *::before, *::after {\n  box-sizing: inherit; /* shows the idea */\n}" },
    { id: 69, level: "intermediate", q: "inherit, initial, unset, revert?", a: "inherit copies the parent. initial is the spec default. unset is inherit for inherited properties, else initial. revert goes back toward the user-agent stylesheet.\n\nUse these when undoing a rule.\n\nIn the code: .plain has color inherit and display revert. Color comes from the parent. display goes back toward the browser default. Two keywords, two jobs.\n\nIf you meant “undo my stylesheet,” prefer revert over initial.", code: ".plain { /* shows the idea */\n  color: inherit; /* shows the idea */\n  display: revert; /* shows the idea */\n}" },
    { id: 70, level: "advanced", q: "inherited vs non-inherited properties?", a: "font, color, and line-height inherit. margin, padding, border, and display do not. That is why color on body styles text, but padding does not trickle as spacing.\n\nDo not expect padding to cascade like color.\n\nIn the code: body sets color and font-family. .box sets padding 16px. Children inherit the color. They do not inherit the padding. Compare the two rules.\n\nSet padding on each box that needs it. Set color once on a parent.", code: "/* Example: inherited vs non-inherited properties? */\nbody { color: #222; font-family: sans-serif; } /* one CSS rule */\n.box { padding: 16px; } /* children do not inherit this padding */\n/* compare the two rules above */" },
    { id: 71, level: "intermediate", q: "line-height unitless?", a: "A unitless line-height factor multiplies each element’s own font-size and inherits as a factor. A pixel line-height stays 24px even if a child is larger text.\n\nNested headings stay proportional.\n\nIn the code: body is line-height 1.5. h1 is font-size 2rem and still 1.5 times its own size. Compare the two rules. The factor inherited, not 24px.\n\nNever set line-height in px on body if headings should grow their own gaps.", code: "/* Example: line-height unitless? */\nbody { line-height: 1.5; } /* one CSS rule */\nh1 { font-size: 2rem; } /* still 1.5 times its own size */\n/* compare the two rules above */" },
    { id: 72, level: "beginner", q: "How do forms send data?", a: "A form posts or gets to an action URL. Successful controls with a name are included. GET puts fields in the query string. POST puts them in the body.\n\nJS can preventDefault for SPA submit. The server receives named fields.\n\nIn the code: form method post action /signup. label for n matches input id n name name. submit button says Go. name=\"name\" is the key sent.\n\nIf a field is missing on the server, it probably has no name attribute.", code: "<form method=\"post\" action=\"/signup\"> <!-- collect input -->\n  <label for=\"n\">Name</label> <!-- name the control -->\n  <input id=\"n\" name=\"name\" /> <!-- type or pick here -->\n  <button type=\"submit\">Go</button> <!-- do an action -->\n</form>" },
    { id: 73, level: "intermediate", q: "GET vs POST in forms?", a: "GET is bookmarkable and cached. Do not use it for secrets or “please change something.” POST is for create/update and sensitive fields. APIs can use more HTTP methods than HTML forms.\n\nSafety depends on the method.\n\nIn the code: search is method get with input name q. login is method post with a password field. Compare the two forms. Search may appear in the URL. Login should not.\n\nNever put a password in a GET form.", code: "<form method=\"get\" action=\"/search\"> <!-- collect input -->\n  <input name=\"q\" /> <!-- type or pick here -->\n</form>\n<form method=\"post\" action=\"/login\"> <!-- collect input -->\n  <input name=\"password\" type=\"password\" /> <!-- type or pick here -->\n</form>" },
    { id: 74, level: "beginner", q: "What is autocomplete on inputs?", a: "autocomplete hints email, current-password, and so on. Browsers and password managers fill faster.\n\nLogin UX depends on the right token.\n\nIn the code: email input has autocomplete email. password input has current-password. Compare the two tags. type matches the hint.\n\nUse new-password on a signup field, not current-password.", code: "<!-- Example: What is autocomplete on inputs? -->\n<input type=\"email\" name=\"email\" autocomplete=\"email\" /> <!-- type or pick here -->\n<input type=\"password\" name=\"password\" autocomplete=\"current-password\" /> <!-- type or pick here -->\n<!-- compare the two tags above -->" },
    { id: 75, level: "intermediate", q: "fieldset and legend?", a: "fieldset wraps related controls. legend names the group. Native and accessible. Use it for “pick one size.”\n\nScreen readers announce the legend with each radio.\n\nIn the code: fieldset has legend Size. two radios share name s, labels S and M. Same name makes one group. legend is the group title.\n\nDo not skip fieldset for a radio group. A heading nearby is not the same.", code: "<fieldset> <!-- shows the idea -->\n  <legend>Size</legend> <!-- shows the idea -->\n  <label><input type=\"radio\" name=\"s\" /> S</label> <!-- name the control -->\n  <label><input type=\"radio\" name=\"s\" /> M</label> <!-- name the control -->\n</fieldset>" },
    { id: 76, level: "intermediate", q: "disabled vs readonly?", a: "disabled is not editable, not submitted, and often skipped in tab order. readonly is submitted but not edited.\n\nIds you must still send should be readonly, not disabled.\n\nIn the code: the first input is Ada disabled. the second is name id value u1 readonly. Compare the two. Disabled Ada is not submitted. readonly id is.\n\nIf the server never gets the id, you probably disabled the field.", code: "<!-- Example: disabled vs readonly? -->\n<input value=\"Ada\" disabled /> <!-- type or pick here -->\n<input name=\"id\" value=\"u1\" readonly /> <!-- type or pick here -->\n<!-- compare the two tags above -->" },
    { id: 77, level: "advanced", q: "shadow DOM?", a: "Shadow DOM is a private DOM and CSS bubble on a web component. Styles do not leak in or out by default. Slots project light-DOM children. ::part can style some insides.\n\nDesign-system widgets use this.\n\nIn the code: XCard extends HTMLElement. constructor calls super, attachShadow open, and innerHTML with a slot. Light children land in the slot. The shadow root is private.\n\nIf your CSS cannot reach inside a component, it is probably shadow DOM.", code: "class XCard extends HTMLElement { /* a blueprint */\n  constructor() { /* shows the idea */\n    super(); /* shows the idea */\n    const root = this.attachShadow({ mode: \"open\" }); /* a named box */\n    root.innerHTML = \"<p><slot></slot></p>\"; /* shows the idea */\n  }\n}" },
    { id: 78, level: "intermediate", q: "custom elements?", a: "Custom elements are tags like <x-card>. class extends HTMLElement, then customElements.define. Names must include a hyphen.\n\nReact interop needs care. Reusable widgets use this.\n\nIn the code: XHi extends HTMLElement. connectedCallback sets textContent Hi. define(\"x-hi\", XHi) registers the tag. The hyphen is required.\n\nIf define uses a name without a hyphen, the browser rejects it.", code: "class XHi extends HTMLElement { /* a blueprint */\n  connectedCallback() { /* shows the idea */\n    this.textContent = \"Hi\"; /* shows the idea */\n  }\n}\ncustomElements.define(\"x-hi\", XHi); /* shows the idea */" },
    { id: 79, level: "beginner", q: "What is the difference between SVG and PNG/JPG?", a: "SVG is math shapes, sharp at any size, great for icons. Photos are raster (jpg/webp/avif). PNG is good for simple transparency.\n\nPrefer SVG for UI icons. Photos stay raster.\n\nIn the code: an svg 24×24 with aria-hidden has a circle fill currentColor. an img photo.jpg has alt A mountain. Icon vs photo. currentColor follows text color.\n\nDo not use a PNG for a simple logo that must stay sharp on a large screen.", code: "<svg width=\"24\" height=\"24\" aria-hidden=\"true\"> <!-- shows the idea -->\n  <circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"10\" fill=\"currentColor\" /> <!-- shows the idea -->\n</svg>\n<img src=\"photo.jpg\" alt=\"A mountain\" /> <!-- picture plus alt -->" },
    { id: 80, level: "intermediate", q: "inline SVG vs img src svg?", a: "Inline SVG can be styled and animated with CSS. img src svg is cacheable and simpler but treated as an image.\n\nTheming icons is easier inline.\n\nIn the code: the first is inline svg.icon with a path. the second is img src icon.svg alt=\"\". Compare the two. Inline can use fill in CSS. img cannot style internals the same way.\n\nIf the icon must change color on hover, inline it.", code: "<!-- Example: inline SVG vs img src svg? -->\n<svg class=\"icon\"><path d=\"M2 2h20v20H2z\" /></svg> <!-- shows the idea -->\n<img src=\"icon.svg\" alt=\"\" /> <!-- picture plus alt -->\n<!-- compare the two tags above -->" },
    { id: 81, level: "intermediate", q: "currentColor?", a: "currentColor equals the computed color. SVG fill: currentColor lets an icon inherit text color.\n\nButtons that change color on hover then recolor their icons too.\n\nIn the code: .btn color is #06c. svg fill is currentColor. :hover color is #003. The icon follows the button text color.\n\nSet color on the button. Let the svg use currentColor. Do not hardcode fill on every state.", code: "/* Example: currentColor? */\n.btn { color: #06c; } /* one CSS rule */\n.btn svg { fill: currentColor; } /* one CSS rule */\n.btn:hover { color: #003; } /* one CSS rule */" },
    { id: 82, level: "advanced", q: "will-change and transforms for animation?", a: "Animate transform and opacity when you can. will-change can promote a layer but wastes memory if overused. Use it only for known jank.\n\n60fps UI prefers compositing properties.\n\nIn the code: .toast starts translateY(0) with a 200ms transition. .toast.hide is translateY(-100%). Motion is transform, not top.\n\nDo not animate top or height if transform can do the same motion.", code: ".toast { /* shows the idea */\n  transform: translateY(0); /* shows the idea */\n  transition: transform 200ms ease; /* shows the idea */\n}\n.toast.hide { transform: translateY(-100%); } /* one CSS rule */" },
    { id: 83, level: "intermediate", q: "transform vs top/left for animation?", a: "top and left can trigger layout. transform usually only composites. Use transform for moving UI.\n\nSmooth motion depends on this.\n\nIn the code: .fly is position relative and transform translateX(8px). The comment says cheaper than left 8px. The box moves on the compositor.\n\nIf a drag feels janky, you are probably writing left/top every frame.", code: ".fly { /* shows the idea */\n  position: relative; /* shows the idea */\n  transform: translateX(8px); /* cheaper than left: 8px */\n}" },
    { id: 84, level: "intermediate", q: "prefers-reduced-motion?", a: "prefers-reduced-motion lets you wrap decorative animation. Offer an instant state. People with vestibular issues asked the OS to reduce motion.\n\nHonor that request.\n\nIn the code: @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) sets animation and transition none !important on *. Motion stops. The query is the hook.\n\nKeep essential fade-ins optional. Never require spinning UI.", code: "@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) { /* shows the idea */\n  * {\n    animation: none !important; /* shows the idea */\n    transition: none !important; /* shows the idea */\n  }\n}" },
    { id: 85, level: "beginner", q: "How do you make a responsive image gallery?", a: "A responsive gallery is CSS grid with minmax, images at width 100% and height auto. object-fit if you crop. srcset for files. meaningful alt. Do not use tiny tap targets.\n\nPhones need large enough tiles.\n\nIn the code: .gallery is grid auto-fit minmax 160px, gap 8px. img is width 100% height auto. Tiles grow and shrink. Images scale with the cell.\n\nAdd alt on each img. This snippet styles the grid, not the HTML.", code: ".gallery { /* shows the idea */\n  display: grid; /* rows and columns */\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(160px, 1fr)); /* column sizes */\n  gap: 8px; /* shows the idea */\n}\n.gallery img { width: 100%; height: auto; } /* one CSS rule */" },
    { id: 86, level: "intermediate", q: "what are tap targets?", a: "Tap targets are clickable size on touch screens. Aim for about 24–44px minimum with spacing. Mobile users miss small links.\n\nButtons and icons need this.\n\nIn the code: .icon-btn is min-width 44px and min-height 44px. The hit area is at least 44px. The icon inside can be smaller.\n\nIf a heart icon is 16px, pad the button, do not shrink the hit area.", code: ".icon-btn { /* shows the idea */\n  min-width: 44px; /* shows the idea */\n  min-height: 44px; /* shows the idea */\n}" },
    { id: 87, level: "intermediate", q: "CSS grid vs table for forms alignment?", a: "Use grid or flex to line up labels. Tables only if the data is a table. Form rows as grid columns keep labels aligned.\n\nReal tables vs fake layout tables matter for accessibility.\n\nIn the code: form is grid, 8rem then 1fr, gap, align-items center. Labels sit in column one. Fields in column two. Rows stay aligned.\n\nDo not wrap a form in table/tr/td just to line up labels.", code: "form { /* shows the idea */\n  display: grid; /* rows and columns */\n  grid-template-columns: 8rem 1fr; /* column sizes */\n  gap: 8px 12px; /* shows the idea */\n  align-items: center; /* shows the idea */\n}" },
    { id: 88, level: "advanced", q: "subgrid?", a: "Subgrid lets a child grid inherit the parent’s column sizes. Nested items then line up with the outer grid. Support is modern; mention fallbacks.\n\nCard rows with aligned titles use this.\n\nIn the code: .outer is 1fr 2fr. .inner is subgrid and grid-column 1 / -1, spanning both columns. Compare the two rules. Inner tracks match outer.\n\nIf subgrid is missing, flatten the HTML or repeat the same template-columns.", code: "/* Example: subgrid? */\n.outer { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr; } /* rows and columns */\n.inner { display: grid; grid-template-columns: subgrid; grid-column: 1 / -1; } /* rows and columns */\n/* compare the two rules above */" },
    { id: 89, level: "intermediate", q: "container queries?", a: "Container queries style based on a parent container’s size, not the viewport. Great for a card that lives in a sidebar or a wide main.\n\nReusable components can respond to their slot, not the whole window.\n\nIn the code: .wrap is container-type inline-size. @container min-width 20rem makes .card a two-column grid. The card reacts to .wrap, not the viewport.\n\nSet container-type on the parent. Query that container, not @media, for component layout.", code: ".wrap { container-type: inline-size; } /* one CSS rule */\n@container (min-width: 20rem) { /* shows the idea */\n  .card { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } /* rows and columns */\n}" },
    { id: 90, level: "beginner", q: "What is the difference between <head> and <body>?", a: "head holds metadata, title, links, and early scripts. body holds visible content. Title is required for usable tabs and bookmarks.\n\nA valid page has both.\n\nIn the code: DOCTYPE, html, head with title Home, body with h1 Hello. The visible Hello is in body. Home is the tab title.\n\nDo not put the main heading only in title. Users need it in body too.", code: "<!DOCTYPE html> <!-- shows the idea -->\n<html> <!-- shows the idea -->\n  <head><title>Home</title></head> <!-- shows the idea -->\n  <body><h1>Hello</h1></body> <!-- shows the idea -->\n</html>" },
    { id: 91, level: "intermediate", q: "canonical and meta description?", a: "canonical points to the preferred URL to avoid duplicate pages. description hints search snippets. Open Graph tags help social cards.\n\nSearch and sharing use these.\n\nIn the code: link rel canonical to example.com/a. meta description is a short sentence. Compare the two tags. Canonical picks the official URL. Description is the blurb.\n\nIf two URLs show the same article, set canonical to one of them.", code: "<!-- Example: canonical and meta description? -->\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://example.com/a\" />\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Learn HTML in easy words.\" /> <!-- info for the browser -->\n<!-- compare the two tags above -->" },
    { id: 92, level: "intermediate", q: "security: target=_blank?", a: "When opening a new tab, use rel=\"noopener noreferrer\". That stops the new page from using window.opener. Modern browsers default noopener on _blank, but be explicit.\n\nPhishing tabs have used opener.\n\nIn the code: a has target _blank and rel noopener noreferrer. External is the link text. href is example.com. The rel list is the safety bit.\n\nDo not use target=_blank without rel noopener noreferrer.", code: "<!-- Example: security: target=_blank? -->\n<a href=\"https://example.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">\n  External /* shows the idea */\n</a>" },
    { id: 93, level: "advanced", q: "Content Security Policy at HTML level?", a: "Content Security Policy lists which scripts and styles may run. You can use a meta tag, but an HTTP header is better. nonce or hash can allow specific inline scripts.\n\nThis helps against XSS.\n\nIn the code: meta http-equiv Content-Security-Policy with default-src 'self'. Only same-origin resources are allowed by that policy. The meta is in HTML.\n\nPrefer a real CSP header from the server. Meta is a fallback.", code: "<meta <!-- info for the browser -->\n  http-equiv=\"Content-Security-Policy\" /* shows the idea */\n  content=\"default-src 'self'\" /* shows the idea */\n/>" },
    { id: 94, level: "beginner", q: "What is the difference between classList and style in JS?", a: "Prefer toggling classes so CSS stays in stylesheets. element.style is inline and high specificity.\n\nDesigners can still change CSS if you use classes.\n\nIn the code: el is querySelector .box. classList.add(\"open\") is the preferred line. style.color = \"red\" is inline and harder to override. Both are shown.\n\nIf you must set a one-off, style is fine. For states, use a class.", code: "/* Example: What is the difference between classList and style in JS? */\nconst el = document.querySelector(\".box\"); /* a named box */\nel.classList.add(\"open\");     // prefer this\nel.style.color = \"red\";       // inline, harder to override" },
    { id: 95, level: "intermediate", q: "how do you implement a dark theme in CSS?", a: "Put tokens on :root and swap them on [data-theme=\"dark\"] or prefers-color-scheme. Persist the user’s choice if you offer a toggle.\n\nNight reading uses a dark palette.\n\nIn the code: :root has --bg white --fg #111. [data-theme=\"dark\"] swaps them. body uses the variables. One attribute changes the whole page.\n\nToggle data-theme on html or body. Do not recolor every component by hand.", code: "/* Example: how do you implement a dark theme in CSS? */\n:root { --bg: #fff; --fg: #111; } /* one CSS rule */\n[data-theme=\"dark\"] { --bg: #111; --fg: #eee; } /* one CSS rule */\nbody { background: var(--bg); color: var(--fg); } /* one CSS rule */" },
    { id: 96, level: "intermediate", q: "logical properties?", a: "Logical properties follow writing direction. margin-inline-start is left in LTR and right in RTL. Use inline/block axes for international sites.\n\nArabic and Hebrew layouts then flip correctly.\n\nIn the code: .icon has margin-inline-start 8px and padding-block 4px. Start follows the sentence direction. Block is top and bottom.\n\nIf you wrote margin-left, RTL would still pad the left wall. Use inline-start.", code: ".icon { /* shows the idea */\n  margin-inline-start: 8px; /* left in LTR, right in RTL */\n  padding-block: 4px; /* space inside */\n}" },
    { id: 97, level: "beginner", q: "What does HTML validation catch?", a: "HTML validation catches unclosed tags, illegal nesting, and missing required attributes. Valid HTML is easier for browsers and assistive tech. Use a validator or linter.\n\nCheck HTML before you blame CSS.\n\nIn the code: a p Hello, a comment not to wrap a div inside a p, and a button type button. The comment is the nesting rule. The tags shown are valid.\n\nIf CSS “does not apply,” inspect whether the browser repaired broken nesting.", code: "<!-- Example: What does HTML validation catch? -->\n<p>Hello</p> <!-- shows the idea -->\n<!-- do not wrap a <div> inside a <p> -->\n<button type=\"button\">OK</button> <!-- do an action -->" },
    { id: 98, level: "advanced", q: "illegal nesting examples?", a: "Do not put a button inside a button, or an a inside an a. Do not wrap a div in a p. React hydration warnings often come from this.\n\nBroken DOM trees cause weird CSS and events.\n\nIn the code: the bad p>div is commented. A div wrapping p x is the legal version. Nested a tags are also commented as bad. The valid tree is div > p.\n\nIf the inspector shows extra tags you did not write, the parser is fixing illegal nesting.", code: "<!-- bad: <p><div>x</div></p> -->\n<div> <!-- shows the idea -->\n  <p>x</p> <!-- shows the idea -->\n</div>\n<!-- bad: <a><a>twice</a></a> -->" },
    { id: 99, level: "intermediate", q: "how would you build a responsive nav?", a: "A responsive nav is a list of links, flex with wrap, or a details/summary hamburger. Visible focus, aria-expanded on a toggle, large tap targets. CSS-only details is a simple pattern.\n\nPhones need the collapsed form.\n\nIn the code: nav contains details, summary Menu, and a ul with Home. details/summary is the disclosure. No JS is required for open/close.\n\nStyle summary as a button-sized hit area. Keep the links in a list.", code: "<nav> <!-- the menu -->\n  <details> <!-- shows the idea -->\n    <summary>Menu</summary> <!-- shows the idea -->\n    <ul> <!-- shows the idea -->\n      <li><a href=\"/\">Home</a></li> <!-- shows the idea -->\n    </ul>\n  </details>\n</nav>" },
    { id: 100, level: "advanced", q: "How do you discuss CSS architecture in an interview?", a: "Organize CSS in layers: reset, tokens, layout, components, utilities. Keep specificity low. Name things predictably. Delete unused CSS. Say who owns what in a design system.\n\nA clever selector is less important than a clear file plan.\n\nIn the code: :root has --space and --text. .layout is grid with gap. .btn uses padding token. .mt-2 is a utility margin. Tokens, layout, component, utility.\n\nPut colors and space on :root. Do not copy 8px into fifty rules.", code: ":root { --space: 8px; --text: #111; } /* one CSS rule */\n.layout { display: grid; gap: var(--space); } /* rows and columns */\n.btn { padding: var(--space); } /* space inside */\n.mt-2 { margin-top: calc(var(--space) * 2); } /* space outside */" }
  ]
};
